AB  - Accurate mapping of individual tree mortality is critical for assessing forest carbon loss, yet dead trees in aerial imagery often appear scattered, irregular, and blurred, causing high omission rates and low precision for conventional semantic segmentation models. To solve this, we propose TreeMortSeg, a tree mortality segmentation network based on multi-task learning. Structure-aware and edge-aware auxiliary learning tasks are introduced to enhance the spatial structure representation and boundary localization of dead trees. These tasks provide effective spatial geometry and contour priors for the primary segmentation task, thereby reducing omission errors and improving segmentation precision. On the TreeFinder dataset, TreeMortSeg achieved the best dead tree F1-score (66.29%) and IoU (49.57%), outperforming the second-best model (DeepLabV3+) by 6.9 and 7.3 percentage points, respectively. Furthermore, it demonstrated strong robustness across diverse forest conditions and superior cross-regional generalizability. This research supports precise large-scale tree mortality mapping and reliable forest carbon loss assessment.
