AB  - Precipitation uncertainty and complex generation processes hinder karst runoff simulation. We developed an integrated modeling framework combining the Robust Dynamic Merging Algorithm (RDMA) with the improved Xin'anjiang (KXAJ) model and evaluated it in Chengbi River Basin. RDMA dynamically merges five satellite precipitation products to generate robust forcing; KXAJ couples saturation-excess and infiltration-excess runoff mechanisms. The merged product (RDMAP) outperforms the best single-source product, improving KGE by 10.96% and reducing MAE by 34.83%. During validation, the framework improves NSE by 5.56% and decreases RMSE by 7.48%, with KXAJ consistently outperforming XAJ. Error attribution shows that precipitation input error contributes the least, confirming RDMA's effectiveness in enhancing temporal consistency, while interaction error dominates, highlighting the need for synergistic improvements in precipitation estimation and model structure. Multi-source uncertainty analysis confirms that KXAJ maintains stable performance and RDMA is insensitive to hyperparameter fluctuations and observational noise, demonstrating strong structural robustness for data-scarce karst basins.
