AB  - Rapid urbanization exacerbates air pollution and endangers public health and economy. Accurate short-term PM2.5 prediction supports pollution control, policy-making and regional environmental governance. Driven by emissions, regional transport and meteorology, PM2.5 exhibits complex nonlinear spatiotemporal patterns. Existing models suffer defects in graph construction, spatiotemporal fusion and long-term temporal modeling. This work presents LGMPST, a spatiotemporal graph network integrating Laplacian probabilistic attention, graph-convolutional up-down mapping and multi-perspective pooling. Tested on 36 Beijing monitoring stations, LGMPST surpasses LSTM, AirFormer, STiTransformer and other advanced baselines. Seasonal comparison, multi-station tests, pollution-stratified evaluation and ablation studies validate its performance. Cross-regional experiments over Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei and Guangzhou further confirm generalizability for refined air-quality forecasting and joint governance.
