AB  - Urban flooding is a growing issue worldwide, requiring high-fidelity, physics-based distributed models to capture flood dynamics at localized scales. Such modeling requires high-resolution spatial (e.g., ≤5m) and temporal data, resulting in substantial computational demands. In addition, hydrodynamic modeling tools, although based on similar physical principles, often require tool-specific data processing, resulting in fragmented data pipelines and workflows that limits scalability and interoperability. We present a scalable, interoperable, and reproducible framework for high-resolution 2D hydrodynamic urban flood modeling using a rain-on-grid approach with publicly available datasets. The framework integrates algorithms for data processing, model setup, and simulation execution, and is validated across watersheds and supports local and cloud computing environments. Case studies in Atlanta, Phoenix, Copenhagen, and Cape Town demonstrate scalability across geographic contexts and spatial resolutions, and interoperability across different hydrodynamic modeling platforms. This can further support infrastructure planning, emergency response, risk assessment, and development of physics informed machine learning models.
