AB  - Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) are a major source of uncertainty in urban flood simulations. This study develops a consensus framework for urban flood risk assessment under DEM uncertainty in densely built-up central Shanghai. Five global DEMs drove TELEMAC-2D simulations under 50- and 100-year rainfall scenarios. A Risk Consensus Index (RCI) was introduced to quantify spatial agreement and identify stable risk zones. DEM suitability was evaluated using historical waterlogging detection, spatial consistency, and uncertainty characterization. Results reveal clear DEM-dependent differences, with Copernicus (COP) DEM producing the largest proportion of high-risk areas. Stable risk zones account for 11.52%–14.39% of the study area and represent priority areas for flood-risk management. Functionally, under the modelling conditions and scenarios adopted in this study, the five DEMs exhibit distinct expansive, sensitive, robust, and conservative risk-identification patterns. The framework supports DEM selection and reliable flood risk identification where high-resolution terrain data are unavailable.
