KW  - Data architecture
KW  - Carbon footprint management
KW  - Urban mobility
KW  - Sustainability-oriented evaluation framework
AB  - Urban mobility decarbonization is not only a technical challenge but also a governance issue, as fragmented data infrastructures across public agencies often constrain coordinated climate action. Despite increasing investments in smart mobility initiatives, many urban information systems remain poorly integrated, limiting data quality, interoperability, and institutional decision-making capacity. This study develops a sustainability-oriented data architecture for urban mobility carbon management, grounded in TOGAF and ISO/IEC 25012 principles. Rather than proposing a new environmental modeling, prediction, or optimization methodology, the study focuses on the data architecture and governance mechanisms required to operationalize such analyses within urban institutions. The proposed architecture integrates heterogeneous datasets from mobility, energy, and environmental domains while embedding governance and sustainability considerations throughout the data lifecycle. To assess its effectiveness, an integrated evaluation framework is applied, comprising the Conceptual Alignment Index (CAI), Conceptual Maturity Index (CMI), Resilience Readiness Index (RRI), and Sustainability Readiness Index (SRI). The results indicate strong conceptual coherence, architectural maturity, and resilience across alternative future scenarios, validated by an independent expert panel comprising academic and government practitioner evaluators, with aggregate scores of CAI 89.39%, CMI 58.50%, RRI 74.64%, and SRI 73.12%. A representative demonstration scenario using operational traffic and emission data from Bandung City further illustrates the practical applicability of the proposed framework within a real institutional context. Beyond technical performance, the findings highlight how integrated data infrastructures can enhance inter-agency coordination, analytical transparency, and evidence-based policymaking in urban mobility governance. This study contributes an integrated evaluation model that links architectural design, institutional alignment, and sustainability readiness, offering a transferable reference for cities seeking to strengthen digital infrastructures in support of long-term decarbonization transitions.
