AB  - Anthropocene Earth systems require advanced modeling to address complex, multi-domain societal challenges. This paper evaluates Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) coupled with Hetero-functional Graph Theory (HFGT) as an extension to traditional System Dynamics (SD). A formalized rubric compares both methodologies across graphical modularity, boundary explicitness, and computational scalability. Using a Mono Lake case study, numerical results demonstrate strict analytical equivalence across three multi-scenario perturbations, yielding normalized Root Mean Square Errors (nRMSE) below 1.15% and Pearson correlation coefficients (r) exceeding 0.999. Building on this baseline equivalence, we hypothesize that the explicit structural rigor and mathematical scaffolding of MBSE-HFGT will overcome scalability limitations to effectively model highly coupled, large-scale Anthropocene Systems of Systems—a theoretical advantage to be tested in forthcoming work.
