AB  - Accurate long-horizon carbon-emission forecasting requires models that capture inter-city dependence, remain robust under recursive prediction, and separate model outputs from policy targets. This study develops a Knowledge-Guided Graph Time-Series (KG-GraphTS) framework for 14 prefecture-level cities in Guangxi, China, using a 2000–2024 panel of socioeconomic, energy, industrial, demographic, remote-sensing, and climate variables. Secondary-industry GDP is incorporated both as a predictor and as structural knowledge for an economic-knowledge graph combining economic gravity, industrial-structure similarity, and geographical proximity. The model integrates graph convolution, a two-layer GRU, city-specific autoregressive residual learning, City-Specific Bias, and soft knowledge-guided regularization. A leakage-controlled chronological design is used for training, recursive validation, and held-out testing, while future emissions are projected under Baseline, Low-Carbon, High-Carbon, and Carbon-Neutrality-oriented scenarios without imposing terminal policy targets. KG-GraphTS achieves R2 = 0.9900 and RMSE = 2.1846 Mt under rolling evaluation and retains R2 = 0.9679 and RMSE = 3.9085 Mt under fixed-origin recursive testing. Under the Carbon-Neutrality-oriented scenario, emissions peak at 455.77 Mt in 2034 and decline to 165.90 Mt by 2060, a 63.6% reduction, yet all cities peak after 2030. Under the Baseline scenario, all cities reach Alert by 2030 and cumulative emissions exceed the Carbon-Neutral reference budget by 15.46 Gt CO2. These results support robust target-independent forecasting and transparent scenario-based decision support with explicit uncertainty and pathway diagnostics.
