AB  - Medium- and long-term scheduling for inter-basin water transfer projects is a high-dimensional, multi-constraint optimization problem. Existing solution methods are constrained by the mathematical properties of the scheduling model and computational efficiency, failing to meet the practical engineering needs. Therefore, based on the simulation–optimization approach (SIM) and analysis of the over-capacity flow (OF) relationships, this study proposes a constraints handling algorithm (OF-SIM) comprised of over-capacity flow traceability, over-capacity flow reduction, and dynamic boundary adaptation. OF-SIM is validated on the Yangtze-to-Huai Water Transfer Project scheduling model, which minimizes water deficit and surplus water across wet, normal and dry years. Results show average reductions (pooled standard deviations) of 98.49 million m3 (1.2 million) in water deficit, 114.64 million m3 (1.49 million) in surplus water, and 20.61 s in runtime across all scenarios compared to the alternate projection correction algorithm and forward-reverse simulation.
