KW  - Multi-criteria decision analysis
AB  - Agriculture is highly dependent on water and energy resources, while resource uncertainties and compound shortages increasingly challenge sustainable food production in watersheds. To address correlated resource risks in agricultural water–energy–food nexus (WEFN) systems, this study develops a vine-copula-based framework integrating joint-risk characterization, interval chance-constrained optimization, and multi-criteria scenario evaluation. The proposed framework represents water and agricultural energy as resource availability constraints and production inputs, with crop sown area as the primary decision variable. An entropy–AHP–TOPSIS approach was applied to evaluate representative scenarios. Application to the Jiulong River Basin, covering four administrative regions at an annual resolution from 2017 to 2023, considers 24 compound-risk scenarios. Results show that integrated system risk exceeds individual subsystem risks but remains below their sum. Increasing violation probability expands feasible allocation ranges and increases the lower-bound sown area by 2.4%. The framework provides risk-informed support for enhancing agricultural sustainability in coastal watersheds.
