AB  - Physics-based hydrodynamic models are the standard approach for urban flood simulation but remain computationally prohibitive for real-time modelling. Although machine learning surrogate models offer a promising alternative, most existing approaches rely on private or simplified datasets, limiting reproducibility and rigorous comparison, particularly for coupled 1D–2D flood systems. To address this gap, we introduce UrbanFloodBench, the first open benchmark for controlled coupled 1D–2D urban flood simulator emulation. UrbanFloodBench combines a multi-catchment HEC-RAS-derived dataset, a variance-aware evaluation framework, and a reproducible large-scale AI challenge. The Kaggle competition evaluated water-level prediction for two simplified, uncalibrated HEC-RAS benchmark catchments under spatially uniform synthetic rainfall, attracting 264 teams and 3799 submissions worldwide. Results show that multiple modelling approaches can achieve strong performance, while the 1D drainage component remains the primary unresolved challenge. UrbanFloodBench establishes a reproducible foundation for advancing AI-based surrogate modelling and future evaluation of data-driven flood-emulation methods.
