AB  - Planning forest biomass-to-energy systems requires integrating spatial resource data, supply chain logistics, technoeconomic modeling, and environmental impact assessment across large, heterogeneous landscapes. Existing tools typically address these components in isolation, limiting their ability to capture the coupled dynamics that determine project feasibility. Part I of this work introduced the Forest Resource and Renewable Energy Decision Support System (FRREDSS), which demonstrated the feasibility of web-based integrated assessment for the Sierra Nevada region. This paper presents FRREDSS 2.0, which extends that foundation with substantial new computational methods for statewide analysis, unifying six interlinked models within a scalable framework to evaluate forest biomass energy pathways across California. The system implements a two-stage cluster processing algorithm combining expanding-radius spatial search with cost-optimized greedy selection to solve annual feedstock procurement problems over 20-year planning horizons. A data transformation pipeline aggregates approximately 1 billion raster pixels from the C-BREC statewide biomass dataset into 2.1 million harvest clusters, each characterized by biomass availability, terrain attributes, and road access. Harvesting costs are computed via an enhanced Fuel Reduction Cost Simulator supporting 11 silvicultural treatments and 10 harvesting systems, and transportation distances are determined using the Open Source Routing Machine with a custom log-truck profile. Parallel execution of spatial queries and model computations achieves sub-second response times. A case study of a 25 MW facility in Butte County demonstrates the system’s ability to capture spatial depletion dynamics, with delivered feedstock costs rising from $42.50 to $133.35/BDT over the facility lifetime and a levelized cost of electricity of $142.72/MWh. FRREDSS 2.0 provides forest managers, energy developers, and policymakers with a unified platform for spatially explicit evaluation of biomass utilization strategies aligned with California’s renewable energy and forest resilience objectives.
