AB  - Climate model output carries systematic biases that limit its use in impact assessment, and conventional correction methods rest on statistical assumptions that constrain how nonlinear relationships are represented. This study evaluates the Residual Correction Generative Adversarial Network (RCGAN), developed in our previous work, for bias correction of relative humidity, precipitation and temperature from five CMIP6 models over China at monthly and daily resolution. Quantile Delta Mapping (QDM) and a CNN-based correction (CNNBC) serve as benchmarks, and all results refer to the 2006–2014 validation period. For monthly data, the relative improvement in Taylor Skill Score (TS) is approximately 77.8% for relative humidity, 26.7% for precipitation and 5.4% for temperature; the corresponding daily values are 114.9%, 26.1% and 9.5%. RCGAN attains a higher TS than QDM and CNNBC for relative humidity and precipitation, whereas for temperature it is only slightly better than either. The improvement therefore depends on the variable corrected.
