AB  - Forecasting river inflows remains a major challenge in hydrology due to the complexity of underlying physical processes, and existing deep learning models often fail to incorporate physical laws or capture hierarchical spatial dependencies. This study introduces TideNet, a physics-aware spatiotemporal forecasting framework that integrates Graph Neural Networks, Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks, Gated Recurrent Units, and Long Short-Term Memory modules. TideNet models the catchment as a dynamic graph in which edges encode slope-aware, hierarchical sub-catchment relationships, while KAN-based node updates capture nonlinear damping more effectively than multilayer perceptrons. Hydrological consistency is enforced through a mass-conserving message-passing mechanism, and 5-day-ahead inflows are predicted using an LSTM optimized with a custom AdaptiveFlow loss that prioritizes peak timing and magnitude. TideNet improves forecasting accuracy by 6% over leading spatiotemporal GNNs and 13% over temporal baselines, while remaining robust under sparse and noisy conditions, advancing physically consistent inflow forecasting for proactive flood management and climate-resilient reservoir operations.
ER  - 
